>> title: Patia
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: prawn, parsi, tamarind, jaggery, curry
>> counters: Curry House
>> aka: patio, prawn patia, patiya, sali patia, prawn pathia, pathia
>> pairs-with: onion-tomato-masala, pilau-rice, papadom
>> servings: 4
>> time: 45 min
>> step.1: blend 2 min to a wet paste
>> step.2: fry 3 min, until it darkens and stops smelling of vinegar
>> step.3: simmer 15 min, until it is thick and glossy
>> step.4: simmer 4 min, until they curl and turn opaque

Blend @dried Kashmiri chiles{8}(stemmed, soaked 20 minutes in hot water), @garlic{6%cloves}, @cumin seeds{1%tsp} and @cane vinegar{2%Tbs}(30 mL) in a #small blender{} ~stir{2%min} to a wet paste.

Fry @&(~1)chile paste{} in @neutral oil{3%Tbs}(45 mL) in a #wide pan{} ~fry{3%min}, until it darkens and the vinegar smell burns off.

Simmer @onion-tomato masala{1%cup}(240 g; the base recipe), @jaggery{2%Tbs}(30 g; or dark brown sugar), @tamarind concentrate{1%Tbs}(20 g), @water{1/2%cup}(120 mL) and @kosher salt{1%tsp} with @&(~1)fried paste{} ~simmer{15%min}, until it is thick and shines.

Simmer @large prawns{1%lb}(450 g; peeled and deveined, tails on), @cilantro{3%Tbs}(chopped) and @mint{1%Tbs}(chopped) in @&(~1)patia{} ~simmer{4%min}, and stop the moment they have curled and gone opaque.

Sweet, sour and hot are all three turned up at once here, which is what tells a patia from a dopiaza that got jam in it — the jaggery, the tamarind and the chile paste are meant to arrive together and separately. Parsi, like dansak, and prawns are what it is printed against, though chicken patia is on plenty of boards. Sali patia comes with a handful of matchstick potatoes on top, fried crisp.
